The project runs agricultural training programmes to offer psychosocial training, vocational livelihood and business skills. The objective is to reduce local and regional armed violence and to achieve peaceful communities in areas where the potential for violence and return to conflict is high.

The objectives of the agricultural training programmes are as follows:
• Armed Violence reduction: Engagement in armed violence, the threat of a return to conflict or recruitment of fighters to regional conflicts are removed in UNMIL-defined ‘hot spot’ areas;
• Beneficiaries are removed from a cycle of poverty and violence;
• Crime and violence in hotspot areas is significantly reduced;
• Beneficiaries have a legal and practical livelihood alternative to a reliance on illegal activities;
• Beneficiaries are economically and socially reintegrated into communities.

To meet these objectives, AoAV will:
• Peacefully disengage groups in illegal hotspots;
• Provide beneficiaries with practical vocational skills in agriculture, numeracy, literacy and business planning to successfully achieve the MOA certificate of achievement following examination;
• Provide trainees with specialized

There are about 40 agreements in completion phase. Closure of these agreements has been affected by shortage of staff and non compliance by implementing partners to contractual obligations. This work will involve following up the status of the outstanding agreements and prepare completion document. Specifically the assignment will:
a)	Establish the status of outstanding agreements;
b)	Collect all relevant documents and reports needed for closure of agreements;
c)	Prepare completion documents;
d)	Update PTA and close agreements in PTA.

The proposal was selected for funding after a call for proposals launched by RNE.
Goal
To contribute to peace building and reconstruction in Northern Uganda

Purpose
The purpose of this project is to improve individual livelihoods and support local economic recovery in Amuru, Oyam and Gulu districts through Cash-for-Work (CFW) infrastructure rehabilitation and small cash grants to support the establishment of Income Generating Activities (IGA) among returning populations as well as EVIs in former IDP camps.

Outputs
•	Best practices/Lessons Learned on CFW and Cash Grants collected;
•	USD 274,580 of cash transferred to 8,500 beneficiaries for CFW rehabilitation works; 
•	50 grants totaling USD 250,000 of cash transferred to 50 IGA groups (including USD50,000 to 10 all-EVI groups); 
•	CFW workers able to carry out future infrastructure maintenance or employed on other rehabilitations; 
•	200 km of roads rehabilitated or maintained;
•	Total of 15 facilities (in schools, health centers and sub-county offices) rehabilitated;
•	Local access to rehabilitated social infrastructure and to farm land along rehabilitated roads improved;  
•	At least 50 IGA created by a total of

